# Assignment 2-3 Part B – IDE Features Reflection

## Introduction

Using the IDE workspace was relatively simple and easy to get the hang of overall. However, the formatting of the instructions, trying to access the correct instructions and understand what was required of me within this assignment, was quite frankly, unreasonably complicated. The sheer amount of extra instructions and how to navigate where the actual rubric is to see the requirements was a bit overdone. However, using Codio made it very simple for me to write to code to complete this assignment, through to color coding of different commands, the auto-filling of code, and clear ways to test multiple codes.

## Feature 1 – Color Coded Commands

Having the different command types be color coded was a real asset to me when creating the code for this assignment. Being able to see the different colors for different commands within each line really helped with finding errors in the lines of code or seeing where the inputs and outputs directly correlate. Having the different parenthesis be different colors in lines also helped with seeing where multiple inputs interact with one another.

## Feature 2 – Auto-Filled Code Lines

Having lines of code auto-filling while typing the code helped to orient my code in ways that made sure they would run smoothly with the Codio system. It certainly helped with limiting errors with the orientation of my code, and making sure it was not only ordered properly, but I wasn't missing any parenthesis or extra spaces.

## Feature 3 – Code Testing

The biggest plus with the IDE is the ease of testing my code. Being able to easily run the code on the left-hand side while seeing my code interact and the outputs on the bottom really made it simple to diagnose and debug the issues within my code. Having the ability to easily run the code without creating the code in one site and having to transport it over was a big positive.

## Conclusion

Using the Codio interface will definitely improve my confidence in my coding abilities. Sorting through all the other instructions and resources is a bit of a time consumer, but the actual virtual interface is very fun and easy to use. Having the commands be color coded really helps with seeing where different commands come from, and where they end. The use of autofill when typing lines of code is a big asset to see how the interface reads the code, and how to create the code to work with help from the actual interface. Finally, the ease of testing the code within the IDE is a big plus, as it makes me work more efficiently and smoothly between debugging, testing, typing commands, and repeat.
